	<TIER TIER_ID="default" LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="default-lt" />
	<TIER TIER_ID="SPEAKER_00" LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="default-lt">
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a2" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts2" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts3">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Well, in nineteen ninety five or six, I forget, I published a paper on Task Is Truth Definition in a philosophical journal.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a5" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts8" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts9">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>In the mid eighties, I published a paper The Proceedings of the Aristotelian Society, that's a philosophical journal.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a6" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts10" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts11">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>My task is truly definition.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a7" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts12" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts13">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>More or less any Englishspeaking philosopher with an interest in tasking will know that journal and what's in it.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a8" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts14" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts15">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>So I found immediately I started getting I got labelled as a task person among philosophers.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a9" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts16" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts17">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>As a task desperate somehow, right?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a10" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts18" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts19">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Okay.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a11" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts20" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts21">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And I get sent quite a lot of stuff, including papers to referee and request to comment on this level.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a12" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts22" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts23">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I notice that English speaking philosophers as a community tend to make what seem to me to be some systematic mistakes in their reading of tasking.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a13" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts24" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts25">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I'm just wondering in the light of this experience, I should jot down some notes under the title Reading Tasking Notes for Philosophers or something like that.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a14" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts26" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts27">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I don't know if I'll get time to do it, that's the only problem.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a15" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts28" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts29">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>If I do get time, I think it would be a worthwhile thing.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a16" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts30" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts31">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>If I could just say a bit about the kind of mistakes which are made, there are two basic kinds of mistakes.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a17" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts32" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts33">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>The first mistake is made by people who don't realize that some things which Tarsky says are part of a mathematical program.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a18" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts34" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts35">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>A part of a mathematical mathematical program program If they knew the program, they would see at once why these remarks fit into how these remarks fit into place and they wouldn't look for other interpretations of them, which they often do.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a19" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts36" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts37">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And the second thing is that this is my own estimate Englishspeaking philosophers as a tribe seem quite unusually bad at getting into other people's minds and to do that you've got to talk to a person or read what they say and get some idea of their style and the kind of things they're likely to say or mean.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a20" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts38" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts39">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And somehow this approach is completely foreign to analytical philosophy where you've got to take statements as somebody says something you've got to regard that as a thesis and you've got to tear it to pieces.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a21" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts40" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts41">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah It doesn't leave much room for understanding what on earth they're talking about Attack is immediately aggressively to take it apart Yes there's a very good example of this in a fairly recent book by Echemendi on the nature of logical deduction where he's a little bit guarded because some people sat on him I think but guarded but essentially he complains in one of his early chapters that Tasky committed a thing which he calls Taski's fallacy and the fallacy consists of misplacing a modal operator in an absolutely key step in Taske's paper on logical deduction and the rest of the book is devoted to sorting out what Taske should have said And you think Well I actually read what Taski said and it was clear that although he I say modal operator Taski says must, something must happen Although he says must the modal force of that word is completely ignored in everything which follows in his argument and is obviously irrelevant.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a22" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts42" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts43">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>If you just read through the argument you see it's irrelevant but also if you look more carefully at Tasky's style and see the kinds of words that he uses he never had to touch wood on this because there's an awful lot of Taski but his key words of works of philosophical import are contained in one volume together and he never rests any argument on the meaning of possible or necessary or must or these noble words.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a23" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts44" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts45">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Just repeat quickly first all what you said in the very beginning because he's that just the problem about English philosophers English speaking philosophers as I said in the mid eighties I published an article on task is truth definition the proceedings of the Aristotelian Society and this got me labeled as a tasky person in the English speaking philosophical community.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a24" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts46" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts47">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Also among Poles but the things that I get from Poles are usually very helpful and lots of factual information but among them I now get a fair amount of stuff to referee or with requests for comments I have a picture of that there is a lot of interest at the moment among Englishspeaking analytic philosophers in Taski's methodological views in the nineteen thirties and I was commenting that there seem to be some to me to be some systematic mistakes through all this philosophical literature as commented that there are two things that the philosophers are particularly bad at, which seem to be the chief causes of the problem one is that the philosophers it's not their fault it's not their field but they tend not to realize when Taske makes a remark which well when Taske is doing something that's part of a mathematical program and some remark that he makes is completely explained by that program but they don't know the program and they have to make guesses at what he must have meant so they don't get it When they guess by the way they usually guess that he this must be the result of some conversation between Taski and Hwine This is a standard move But that's not I'm exaggerating a little bit here but the other thing I said is that the methods of debate in analytical philosophy are not conducive to taking seriously other people's intentions or to understanding what people mean by looking at the kind of thing they say in general, their style and their approach You take a thesis at a time, a couple of sentences and you hammer it And do you think that's what Etch Mendi did yes he's a pretty good example of both things So are most of the things that reach me So I think HMND is worse than most people and I think he's more rigid than most philosophers If somebody points out to him something that doesn't fit with his views this is a challenge to defend his views, it's not a piece of evidence which fills in a larger picture.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a26" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts50" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts51">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I've read his thesis on which the book is based I never quite understood the point of it and he kept trying to so I thought it was my fault that I was missing something I did So what do you think now?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a27" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts52" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts53">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Well the way I read it I wasn't I didn't find it much persuasive as I say there was some idea that there's some sort of program of semantics for natural language and that people who think that what Tarski did provides a foundation for that are wrong.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a28" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts54" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts55">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Oh yes he's this I think most people agree with him about that the program in question is Davidson's Davidson does play fast and loose and tasky But then there is another business about what the definition the model theoretic definition of logical consequences is not the right definition of logical consequences Is that what he's saying?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
