		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a29" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts56" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts57">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And that I never understood It needs a lot of unpicking and I corresponded with him a bit and he said some strange things so I pressed it to the point where he said some utterly absurd things but there were things about what so and so had meant girdle or coin at that point I gave up because there wasn't anything if he was prepared to take seriously the view that Girdel had mistakenly thought He got too silly Did you ever review each of these I was talking to a philosopher just at the beginning of this conference who said that he thought Ecchemendi had irritated a lot of people With this particular word?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a30" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts58" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts59">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes it's rather aggressively written and does have rather an air of taking everybody else for a fool This is completely confidential but when Echemeni was being promoted to tenure you wrote a letter which I read of course and a number of other people wrote letters and all those other letters were extremely supportive and people didn't know what to make of your fact most people didn't know who you were.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a31" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts60" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts61">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I had to tell them that this was to be taken seriously but I've wondered many times since then if I was unfair I don't think that his work has in any way improved since I wrote that letter but a person can achieve something important in a field by sticking to his guns and resisting all comers.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a32" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts62" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts63">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I think what needs to be seen is whether he can develop any further but what he's done since then has nothing to do with I mean he's worked with Barwas and well there programs Turing's World, Tarsky's World Hypercrew Diagrammatic Reason It's all applied logic I found Well the term difficult to write Then there was the book The Liar Which was one of the things I based my letter on Now that I hadn't remember that I was Well I had long talks with Barwest my view that they hadn't accomplished what they thought they had but that was clear That's exactly what I that was my main comment But they shouldn't have claimed they were going to accomplish that it's a silly claim in the introduction Right That's a very useful little book and it's beautiful.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a33" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts64" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts65">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes I do suspect that the beautiful things in it are likely to be Barwise rather than Echemandi, but I don't know that they have and I've never seen any evidence that Echemandi is capable of the kind of insight that wove Peter Axel in, for example I was query Barwise's appreciation sorry Barwise's appreciation of how Axel's work could be used Well Barwise I think working on trying to use Axel Set theory and foundations of situations theory situations so it came from that but I guess this is all getting a few that's all right that's okay from what other people have said to me my impression is that philosophers by and large take Picci Mendi more seriously as an authority than I do and I don't take him as any kind of authority on anything and I was rather appalled to see his name suggested as one of the program committee for the nineteen ninety six European meeting in a section on history and philosophy of logic because although I think I had no qualms about him going forward in the program committee in philosophy to see him doing anything in history would scare me a bit he's just more competent and philosophers on the whole tend to regard him as more of an authority than I would, but on the other hand the philosophers that I've talked to can see less that they agree with in what he says than I do perhaps He's a person everybody loves to disagree with I'm very, very interested to hear in some way it's a burden off me to hear a response here because I've been carrying this with me.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
