			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a53" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts104" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts105">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Have you written other things about Tarski Yes but has anything been published We have yards and yards of reports on papers some of which involve going into a lot of detail and some points like referee to referee referee My tutor asked me if I would This is David Weakins who's just going to the chair in logic in Oxford He's been becoming chairs Yes they were trying all over the place for this Is that the Yes they offered it to Kid Thin and he hovered and then refused and offered it to Wiggins I think I'm not sure and he's just coming up to his sixtieth and some of his students decided to write a volume in his honour And we get his Yes and they invited all his old students to contribute and I said I'd love to very fond of him and I'm enormously grateful to them What can I possibly write about a philosophical volume?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a54" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts106" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts107">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>So they asked him to think of something that he would like me to write on and this Davidson say as expected came up but he also sent an article that he took on the question whether moral truths whether there can in any proper sense be moral truths and quite a lot of the article is about Tarski's notion of truth Now that's your article That's your article for the big Wiggins No Wiggins article No Wiggins This is an article that Wiggins wrote maybe ten years ago and he wanted me to comment Essentially what he does is to take the text of Taski and interpret Taski's real motivation and tie it in with his own and emerge with some kind of David Sonyian recasting of Taski's convention T then apply this to moral judgments So I looked at it and tried to write down what I thought were the correct things to say about those things in Taski that he'd been looking at.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
